A1. Greetings to our puzzle public. ~ MERRYCHRISTMAS
A13. Present? Yes. Who for? Be. Can she use it? No. Can they use it? Yes. ~ ARE
A14. After Christmas you will be able to get this for a song. ~ ARIA
A15. It will be hard to find this kind of a person on Christmas day. ~ IRATE
A16. Heads down. ~ NOD
A17. This reminds one of the Christmas spirit of long ago. ~ PORT
A18. A joint partner of A. Mortise. ~ TENON
A19. Exists for everyone during the holidays. ~ IS
A20. A backward step (for she who foolishly does it). ~ PETS
A22. Do not give this one a copy of "Show Boat" for Xmas. She has the original MSS. ~ EDNA
A23. This might pierce your very sole. ~ AWL
A26. The cut-up at the Xmas dinner. (This is a raw one.) ~ SLAW
A28. This might explain the time left to buy that forgotten present. ~ YET
A29. A little gift for sister—nothing to speak of. ~ UNDIES
A32. You may be sure this "hunk of cheese" will always be round when dinner is ready. ~ EDAM
A34. A long distance call. ~ SOS
A36. Brother will be going some if he gets this for Xmas. ~ ROADSTER
A38. Make this an empty space. ~ GAP
A40. One of the shining lights in ancient history. ~ RA
A41. A lawgiver of long ago ~ MOSES
A42. An old fashioned court house. ~ FORUM
A44. A representative of one of the ocean liners. ~ SS
A46. No matter what happens this will never be lost. ~ WON
A47. Abridge (in more ways than one). ~ CONTRACT
A49. A little article we cut out of the newspaper. ~ THE
A51. The first one to save for a rainy day. ~ NOAH
A53. Because Dad eats so much at dinner time he usually has to take this after the meal. ~ SIESTA
A54. These men were corkers. ~ END
A56. There will undoubtedly be a lot of kicking about this. ~ GRID
A58. This is work of the radio. ~ NET
A59. Many a yarn has been woven about this. ~ LOOM
A61. What someone did to the fruit cake. ~ ICED
A63. When this starts flowing there will be no more owing. ~ FL
A64. The kind of a spotlight Santa Claus uses. ~ LUNAR
A66. Scotch soup (and of course savory). ~ KAIL
A68. Some brother is entitled to this. ~ FRA
A69. This usually follows the mince pie. ~ AGONY
A70. If this seems difficult try and make it otherwise. ~ ELSE
A71. One of the girls. ~ LOU
A72. A lovely gift from Dad to Mom—six pairs of full yellow bloomers. ~ CHRYSANTHEMUMS

D1. The first one that nailed Andy. ~ MANICURIST
D2. That mistletoe game may date back to this. ~ EROS
D3. This helps to make a bright Xmas. ~ RED
D4. Backward pay. ~ YAP
D5. Where the gobble, gobble of the turkey goes. ~ CROP
D6. What dad does for the help in the kitchen on Xmas. ~ HIRES
D7. What Hor. 36 will be a year from this Xmas. ~ RATTLER
D8. If the wets will not stand for prohibition this is what they will have to do. ~ SIT
D9. After the holiday season this is a needleless article. ~ TREE
D10. Here's a dandy Name is —— Makes plum-cake and sugar candy. ~ MANDY
D11. What Junior always does before Xmas ~ ATONES
D12. If this one does not get a muffler for Xmas it will be just too bad. ~ SENATOR
D21. When you feel this way tell it to the "Judge." ~ SAD
D23. What a man! So original! ~ ADAM
D24. This was presented to the bachelor guest by the hostess (no ties). ~ WIDOW
D25. What the professor gave to pretty Betty Coed. ~ LESSON
D27. Some of these are water carriers—you will catch on when you make your New Year's resolutions. ~ WAGONS
D30. This has always been a trump. ~ NO
D31. Mother likes to select this for father. ~ STENOG
D33. A bird that led a flock of cardinals to victory. ~ MARTIN
D35. "King for a day." ~ SANTACLAUS
D37. If you have the cloth this will make clothes for you. ~ ES
D39. We hope no one hears this at the Xmas dinner. ~ PUREE
D42. This is all it takes to get folks started. ~ FO
D43. A boom might come from this. ~ MAST
D45. A coat of this gets so shiny. ~ SHELLAC
D47. This one simply lost her head and heart at the Xmas party. ~ CHICKEN
D48. Look out for short change here. ~ CT
D50. When you get this let us know. Some people never get it. ~ ENOUGH
D52. Here is where Ira got in wrong. ~ ARI
D55. The one who was responsible for its going into receivers' hands. ~ DONOR
D57. Gave the big hand to each one of the company. ~ DEALT
D60. We wish you this number of happy returns of the day. ~ MANY
D62. If this is dropped it may mean the breaking up of China. ~ DISH
D63. Just a word between the receiver and giver. ~ FROM
D65. Chicago is full of them (abbr.). ~ RYS
D67. Wind shield. ~ LEE
D68. We hope no one gives this to you for Xmas. ~ FLU


